---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About realfileeditor — Why We Built a File Editor That Asks for the Target Size"
  description="realfileeditor is a free online file editor and file compressor built around one idea: you should be able to set the exact output size instead of guessing at a preset."
  path="/about"
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 py-16">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--accent)] mb-3">About</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-8">Why we built a file editor that asks for the target size</h1>

    <div class="space-y-5 text-[var(--fg-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Most online file editors and file compressors work the same way: you pick "low, medium or high"
        quality, press a button, and hope the result fits whatever limit you actually needed — an email
        attachment cap, an upload portal, a Discord server's file-size ceiling. If it doesn't fit, you
        run the tool again and guess differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        realfileeditor flips that around. Every compressor here starts from the number you actually
        care about — the file size — and works backward to hit it, instead of starting from a quality
        preset and leaving the size to chance.
      </p>
      <p>
        We started with PDFs and images because they're the formats people hit size limits with most
        often, and we built each tool to run entirely in your browser: nothing uploads to a server until
        you choose to download the result, and processed files are removed automatically afterward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The toolkit is still growing. You can see exactly which tools are live and which are on the way
        on the homepage — we'd rather ship a smaller set of tools that genuinely work than a long list of
        placeholders.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
